00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.200
What is Site
Reliability Engineering?

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.490
What is an SRE?

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:52.160
PAUL NEWSON: So Site
Reliability Engineering

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.650
is what Google calls
the operations function.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:02.690
And to once again quote Ben
Trainer, like I did last time,

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.330
it's what happens when you put
a software engineer in charge

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.390
of your operations function.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:14.040
And so what you end up with
is a culture of automation

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:22.530
and a culture of using software
to make the operations problem

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:25.830
better or easier or faster.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.920
And as I touched on
in the last episode,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.200
there are some really
important insights to this,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.260
because when you operate
at very high scale,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.990
if you try and scale
the number of people

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.860
taking care of your stuff
with the amount of stuff

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.400
that you have, it
quickly becomes

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.280
an out-of-control problem.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.620
So instead what
you do is you hire

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:51.900
people who are really
good at automating things.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.330
And yes, you do some
manual work along the way,

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.030
but your primary job-- the
thing you spend more than half

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:58.800
of your time on--

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.690
is automating that toil,
as we call it, away.

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:06.461
So those are sort of the two
halves of Site Reliability

00:11:06.461 --> 00:11:06.960
Engineering.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.380
There's a certain amount of
keeping Google up, responding

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.910
to incidents, being on call,
and then the majority of the job

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.270
is building software to
make the services easier

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.640
to run so that as you
double your customers

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.500
and double again
and double again,

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:24.870
and you add more features
and add more features,

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.320
and you increase the surface
area of this product,

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.830
you don't also have to double
the size of your operations

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:34.590
teams again and again and
again, because that just

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:35.792
becomes impossible to do.

00:11:35.792 --> 00:11:38.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So it's kind
of like that thing of go away

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.860
or I will replace you
with a simple shellscript,

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.327
but to yourself.

00:11:42.327 --> 00:11:43.410
PAUL NEWSON: Yes, exactly.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:44.790
You want to automate
yourself out

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.070
of a job as quickly
as possible so

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:49.650
that you can go on to something
newer and interesting.

00:11:52.158 --> 00:11:55.110
A common characteristic of
many Site Reliability Engineers

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.330
at Google is they
get bored very easy.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:59.820
So, you know, you do something
once, you do it twice,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.350
and then you write a script.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.480
It's a very common
thing in our industry.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.960
It's not unique
to Google at all,

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:10.410
but we do try to live
up to that, sort of,

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:11.970
way of doing things.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.580
Because for us, it's
not just a case of well,

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.620
I don't want to do
it a third time,

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:19.770
it's a case of by the time you
have to do it a third time,

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:22.720
you've got these other things
that are piling up, as well.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.770
So you want to automate these
things as quickly as you can,

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.910
otherwise, pretty soon you won't
be able to keep up with it.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:31.810
You just won't be able to
do it manually anymore.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:34.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah,
that is very interesting.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.340
We covered that in the
previous episode, episode 38.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.840
38, 34?

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:40.540
MARK MANDEL: 38.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:40.880
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah.

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:41.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: 38.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:42.720
PAUL NEWSON: I feel like
I'm repeating myself

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:43.710
for a lot of this.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:45.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, but
it is very interesting.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:49.080
And I guess that if people
are interested on what

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.500
is the whole
philosophy behind SRE,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:55.441
they should just go
listen to that episode.

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:56.940
MARK MANDEL: This
is SRE the sequel.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:58.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
this is SRE the SQL.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:04.170
So I guess you've been an
SRE for a bunch of months--

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.055
six months, eight months.

00:13:06.055 --> 00:13:07.680
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah,
it was eight months.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.130
And I'm still wrapping
up a couple of projects

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.160
for the SRE team I was on.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:16.980
So by the time I'm done,
it will have been nine,

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:21.115
10 months of SRE work.

00:13:21.115 --> 00:13:22.540
I'm pretty happy with that.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:23.940
It was a good body of work.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:29.070
It was enough time for me
to really hopefully make

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:32.580
a positive impact on the
team that hosted me, and not

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:36.420
just suck knowledge
out of their heads.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.420
But also to be able to
internalize it and really walk

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:42.600
in their shoes for
long enough to be

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.510
able to represent their
world view to the internet

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:46.890
out there today.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:47.640
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:49.635
So when we first had
you on, you'd been on,

00:13:49.635 --> 00:13:50.760
I think, for about a month.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.593
Now, obviously, you've
been there for eight.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.660
How did the end differ
from the beginning?

00:13:57.660 --> 00:13:59.160
How do you feel
like your impression

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.770
has changed since that time
period of being an SRE?

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.920
PAUL NEWSON: So I
actually went back

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:09.030
and re-listened to the
previous podcast this morning

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:10.960
in the hopes that I
wouldn't repeat myself.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:14.400
Well, that's exactly what I just
did because those thoughts were

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:15.330
in my head.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:19.020
But hopefully they're still
interesting to this audience.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:25.290
But I made some statements
at that time about,

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.540
you know, I'm just new
at this, and I'm mostly

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:28.950
repeating what I've heard.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:30.960
And honestly, a
lot of the things

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:35.860
I said in that previous podcast,
they stand up today quite well.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.850
So if you go back
and listen to that,

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:43.200
not only do I think they still
are quite applicable and quite

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.850
accurate, but I
received feedback

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.090
from some fairly
senior Google SREs

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:52.050
who listened to this podcast,
and they were quite positive.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:54.060
They said, hey, you
really did a good job

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.030
of representing what we do.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.640
And we find we are
often misunderstood,

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.290
not just in the
industry, but even

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:07.860
within Google, as what is the
difference between development

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.025
teams, SRE teams, and
what is traditionally

00:15:11.025 --> 00:15:12.510
thought of as operations.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:18.510
So however, I'd say the
big difference between what

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:21.060
I had experienced at that time
and what I've experienced now

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:25.440
is since then, I got trained up
enough so that I actually went

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.480
on call and carried the pager.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:32.760
So I was the first responder
for a piece of Google Compute

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:39.000
Engine for several weeks since
I spoke to you guys last.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:43.450
And that was a very
interesting experience.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I would
call that, like-- yeah, I

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:46.950
would panic, probably.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:48.491
MARK MANDEL:
Interesting, terrifying.

00:15:48.491 --> 00:15:49.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:52.200
Interesting is a nice
way of putting it.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.640
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah, it's
an interesting experience.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.880
And it can be terrifying.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.470
I got to say, like,
the first time

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:07.170
that I saw my name listed on the
page that shows who's on-call,

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:09.250
that was a little bit surreal.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:12.190
And to be fair, I've
been on call before.

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:14.320
I worked on the
Cloud Storage product

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:18.070
before I joined
Developer Relations.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.550
I was on call for that
product at the time.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.310
But I helped build it, so
I really felt like, well,

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:25.870
throw whatever you want at me.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:29.350
I know how this thing works
from beginning to end.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.537
I can probably troubleshoot
what's going on.

00:16:31.537 --> 00:16:33.370
This was a very different
experience for me,

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:36.010
and is more typical
of the SRE experience,

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:40.870
where you are given something
that somebody else has built,

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:44.170
and yes, you become very
familiar with it over time,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:47.110
but I can't possibly know
it as well as the people who

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:48.250
built it, right?

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:51.430
So I've gone from being on-call
for something that I built

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:53.620
to being on-call for
something I didn't.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:57.610
And that was a little
bit terrifying.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:04.329
But what was very reassuring
was you're not out there alone.

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:07.400
It's not like here's
the pager, good luck.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:08.710
We'll see you in a week.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:09.960
There's an entire team.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:11.410
Yes, you are the
first responder,

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:13.869
but there is an entire
team there to back you up.

00:17:13.869 --> 00:17:18.849
And mostly what you need to do
is apply good judgment on when

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:23.650
to escalate and when to bring
other people in, and also know

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:25.700
what are those paths to do that?

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:31.582
So I was primary
on-call when we had

00:17:31.582 --> 00:17:33.790
what is called an incident,
which is something that's

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:37.570
significant enough that you need
to bring other people into it.

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:42.550
You can't just fix
it quickly yourself.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:44.980
And so I had the
experience of going

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:47.750
through the entire incident
management process.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:49.450
And it really
begins a lot earlier

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:51.670
that when you go on-call.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.660
You do a lot of training,
you learn about your product,

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.310
you do projects in your project,
you shadow other people,

00:17:57.310 --> 00:18:00.520
you are secondary, and then
eventually, you become primary,

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:02.590
and you are on point.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.690
But when you declare
an incident at Google,

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:12.990
there is a tool that
we use to sort of track

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.420
the state and the
status, and show how

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:16.792
are you communicating about it.

00:18:16.792 --> 00:18:19.417
Where do people go if they want
to participate in the response?

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.590
And at no point
during the incident

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.420
was I the one
fixing the problem.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:29.730
It was all about
making sure that people

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:33.000
who could fix the
problem were on the case

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.970
and doing the right things.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:41.010
And then when the
incident was mitigated

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:46.020
and service was back
at 100%, at that point,

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.430
you start a post-mortem.

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:49.530
And this is something I
talked just a little bit

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:53.760
about in the previous interview,
but I now know much more

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.580
deeply, having gone through it.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:00.420
These post-mortems are
incredibly useful things.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:05.710
Google has what we call a
blameless post-mortem culture.

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:09.690
So the idea behind
writing a post-mortem

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.690
is not to figure out
who made a mistake

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:14.550
and then to punish the guilty.

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:16.050
It's actually the
opposite of that.

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.930
You assume that--
we believe that--

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:23.430
the people who were involved
in the incident response,

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.380
the people who were
on call at the time,

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:26.726
are qualified to be there.

00:19:26.726 --> 00:19:28.350
They've been trained
as well as we can.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.610
They are well-meaning
and well-intentioned.

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:32.130
And when things
go wrong, it's not

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:36.600
because people should
have done better.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:38.670
It's because the
environment around them--

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:43.080
the tools, the processes,
the monitoring,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:47.050
the alerting-- these things
somehow could be better.

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:49.830
And if you want to avoid
this problem in the future,

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:52.500
you need to change these
tools, the processes,

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:55.830
or change the product so this
particular type of problem

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:57.930
can never occur again.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.900
And that's what the post-mortem
is supposed to bring out.

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:02.670
And so you write
the post-mortem.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:05.010
Everybody, you know, adds
their thoughts to it.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:08.325
You file a bunch
of follow-up items

00:20:08.325 --> 00:20:09.990
because a document
that nobody reads

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.840
and nobody acts on
is no good to anyone.

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:14.600
These things are
filed and tracked,

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:16.620
the same as any
other product issue.

00:20:19.939 --> 00:20:21.480
And then at some
point, you have what

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.300
we call incident
review meeting, where

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.870
a group of people who work on
that product and the people who

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:28.680
are involved in the
particular incident

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.990
will get down and
get into a room

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:34.560
and review the post-mortem.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.477
And this is also a very
interesting experience

00:20:36.477 --> 00:20:37.934
because it does a
couple of things.

00:20:37.934 --> 00:20:39.540
One, it helps improve
the post-mortem,

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.360
because you have people there
who weren't directly involved,

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:45.360
but who are very experienced
and bring a fresh perspective,

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.554
and they might think
of additional things

00:20:47.554 --> 00:20:49.470
that you didn't think
that we can follow up on

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:52.230
to make the product better.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:55.080
But the second thing it
does is it socializes

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.276
this particular incident
among the responders--

00:20:58.276 --> 00:21:00.150
the people who might
respond, have to respond

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:01.200
to things in the future.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:08.340
And it builds this
culture around we

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:11.160
learn from these problems
we have in the past

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:13.290
to make things better.

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:17.590
And these review meetings are
an important part of that.

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:20.209
So I think the question was
originally what's different now

00:21:20.209 --> 00:21:21.000
from the beginning?

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.430
And I'd say the most
significant difference

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.730
between what I
knew then and what

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:31.260
I know now comes from
living through being on-call

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:34.890
and handling
incidents at Google.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that sounds
like it's very interesting.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:41.640
And for those that do
not have the privilege

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:45.870
of being able to do what you did
and becoming an SRE for almost

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:49.590
a year, I guess that there's
many resources out there.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:53.490
I think that one of my
favorite ones is the SRE book.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.300
I guess you would
recommend that book.

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:57.300
PAUL NEWSON: I would
highly recommend that book.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:02.250
In fact, when I went
through my orientation,

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:05.610
we had some senior SREs
who'd been at the company

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:10.740
doing the SRE role for years
come and have lunch with us.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.440
And one of them said,
yeah, everybody who's

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.000
an SRE should read this book.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:17.250
I learned things from this book.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:20.355
This is somebody who's
been doing it at Google.

00:22:20.355 --> 00:22:23.520
Because the way this book
was put together was it

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.590
was sort of crowdsourced
within the SRE organization.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:29.910
So what you really have
when you have that book

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:35.070
is a collection of
authoritative essays

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.630
written by the person at
Google who knows that subject

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:38.550
the best, right?

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:40.320
So, you know, for
example, there's

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.810
a chapter on post-mortems,
which I just discussed,

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:44.460
and a chapter on
incident response,

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:46.280
which I just discussed.

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:49.440
And they were written
probably by different people,

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.960
and probably by the people
who were best positioned

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.060
to really write the
authoritative treatment

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:54.960
of that subject.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:59.970
Well, nobody at
Google is going to be

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.940
as knowledgeable about all
of the subjects covered

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:05.740
by that book as the individual
authors of each chapter.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:10.380
So longtime SREs at Google get
benefit from reading that book.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:13.950
So yes, I do highly,
highly recommend it.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:17.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So for
those that are lazy--

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.879
too lazy to read
that book, like me--

00:23:19.879 --> 00:23:22.170
I actually have read the book
and I think it's amazing.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.630
But for those that do not have
the time to read the book,

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:28.140
if you were to highlight
one specific theme

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:32.640
or one concept that is very,
very important for an SRE--

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.040
something that you may have
discovered during this year--

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:37.350
what could that be?

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:43.430
PAUL NEWSON: So I think
the most surprising--

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:45.300
and I won't say it
surprised me because it

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:49.110
was a concept I was really
aware of from my previous work

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:50.010
on cloud storage.

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:54.510
But the importance
of having really

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:59.880
well-thought-out
service-level objectives.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.220
The surprise isn't
that that's important.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.950
The surprise to
me was how central

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:07.470
that is to everything
you need to do

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:09.450
to think about reliability.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:12.330
MARK MANDEL: So what is a
service-level objective?

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:17.680
PAUL NEWSON: So let
me back up a second.

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:20.370
A service-level indicator
is something you

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.390
can measure about your service.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:26.220
So for example, let's just
take a simple request response

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:27.660
sort of HTTP service.

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:30.210
A very simple
service-level indicator

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:34.930
is what percentage of my
responses are 500 class?

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:36.650
So error rate.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.990
And, you know, that's
a very relevant metric

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:42.540
that you should probably be
tracking and have on a graph,

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:44.710
and maybe even alert on.

00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:47.850
But what turns a
service-level indicator

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.730
into a service-level objective
is sitting down and asking

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:55.890
yourself, well, for
this indicator, what

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:56.940
is the threshold?

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:58.320
Like what is our goal?

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.720
What are we trying
to reach here?

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.380
So for example, if we're
talking about error rate,

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:06.630
maybe you want to be three 9's.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:13.350
So you're going to give
yourself a goal of 999 times out

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:19.177
of 1,000 you are going to
serve a non-500 response.

00:25:19.177 --> 00:25:20.760
Maybe that's the
goal of your service,

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:23.267
and maybe that's appropriate
for your service,

00:25:23.267 --> 00:25:25.350
but you have to think about
it from the customer's

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:25.933
point of view.

00:25:25.933 --> 00:25:28.440
Like what is that transaction?

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:31.020
What is that HTTP request
you've been given?

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:34.920
Is your service something
where all these HTTP requests

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:38.130
are idempotent and
can be easily repeated

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:40.200
with exponential backoff?

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:44.430
Three 9's is probably very
sufficient for something

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:47.014
like that, because what's
the client going to do?

00:25:47.014 --> 00:25:48.180
What's the user going to do?

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:49.980
Well, the client's
software is probably

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:51.730
going to retry
automatically for them,

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:56.410
and the user will never
notice that a 500 was served.

00:25:56.410 --> 00:26:00.120
So what transforms
an SLI into an SLO

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:03.270
is this user insight that
you have to bring and say,

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:07.980
for this indicator, what
is an acceptable threshold

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:10.650
beyond which we shouldn't go?

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:12.615
Now, a lot of people
would call that an SLA,

00:26:12.615 --> 00:26:15.870
a Service-Level Agreement.

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:21.780
And at Google, we
try very hard to be

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:25.590
clear about what is an SLI,
Service-Level Indicator?

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:27.150
It's just something we measure.

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:29.550
There's a graph
somewhere that you

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:33.360
can go look at that tracks
whatever that metric is.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.460
A service-level
objective is an SLI

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.920
with a threshold of
some kind applied

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.500
to it-- some sort of goal.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:44.820
An SLA is a legal contract
between two companies that

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:46.950
are doing business together.

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:50.860
And they kind of look
like SLOs sometimes,

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:53.520
but really, the SLA
is the contract.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.570
You can have lots of
services that have

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.170
SLOs with no SLA whatsoever.

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:02.590
Like I collaborated with
Philippe, our colleague,

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:06.540
on a post about GitHub
reliability a few months back.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.760
And GitHub is a service.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.366
If you're just using their
free service, they have no SLA.

00:27:11.366 --> 00:27:13.740
It wouldn't make sense to have
a service level agreement.

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:15.448
You're not paying them
any money, how can

00:27:15.448 --> 00:27:16.540
they give you a credit?

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.623
What are they going to do,
like, write you a check

00:27:18.623 --> 00:27:20.090
because they gave you a 500?

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:23.790
No, it that doesn't make sense.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:28.320
But I'm quite sure that
internally at GitHub, they

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:30.950
have objectives for how many
errors they want to serve.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:35.280
They have SLOs even though they
don't have a public-facing SLA.

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:40.050
So the SLO is the thing that
drives your decision-making

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:44.430
around almost everything
you do with your service.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.860
And I'd say that is what I
found the most surprising.

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:48.930
That is the most
valuable insight

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.000
that I've come away
with from this rotation.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.390
You know, let's say,
for example, you're

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:59.990
trying to figure out how many
regions do I need to run in?

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:04.110
And so you can analyze
your architectures

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.200
and say, well, if I run in
multiple zones in one region,

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.890
I can expect this
sort of reliability.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:14.270
If I run in multiple regions,
I can expect more reliability

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:18.440
because I'm now more redundant
across more geographies.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:19.370
Should I do it?

00:28:19.370 --> 00:28:20.850
It's more expensive.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.270
It's more engineering work.

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:27.080
If your SLO demands that you
need that redundancy, then yes,

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:28.160
you should do it.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.980
If your SLO doesn't demand
that you need that redundancy,

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:35.780
then you shouldn't do it because
you're just wasting money.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:38.660
If your SLO is
well-chosen, your customers

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:41.510
will not notice the
difference between meeting

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.210
it and exceeding it greatly.

00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:47.930
There is no extra value to
exceeding your SLO greatly

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:51.350
if you've chosen it well.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:54.650
An example would be
that most people connect

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.830
to internet services through
networks that only provide

00:28:57.830 --> 00:28:59.930
three or four 9's to them.

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:06.710
So while you could provide
five 9's for your website,

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:12.290
that additional reliability
is pretty much imperceptible

00:29:12.290 --> 00:29:13.460
to your customers.

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:16.340
You are spending a lot
of effort and money

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:20.930
providing a higher level of
reliability that provides

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:23.002
no value to your customers.

00:29:23.002 --> 00:29:24.710
Now, there are, of
course, services where

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:25.793
this doesn't apply, right?

00:29:25.793 --> 00:29:29.710
Anything that is life
safety-critical, anything

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:37.490
where failing a request has
an actual cost each time.

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:42.260
They may require a
different calculus

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:44.690
on how to think about your SLO.

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:49.580
But that's something that no
one can decide for you, right?

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:54.290
You understanding your users
and having empathy for them

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:55.940
and thinking about
your product--

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:58.340
only you can choose your
SLO, but once you do,

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:00.530
that can help guide
architectural decisions,

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:03.140
operational decisions,
deployment decisions,

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:05.720
technology decisions,
and so on and so on.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
I think we've had

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:10.240
very interesting discussions
about having SLOs drive, like,

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:12.920
feature development in terms
of how much work should

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:16.530
we put in on this thing because
changes are essentially risk.

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:17.630
PAUL NEWSON: Right.

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:21.697
Yeah, so that's a
very, very good point.

00:30:21.697 --> 00:30:23.780
I think I made the point
in the previous interview

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.570
that if you want to have a
service that is 100% reliable,

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:27.380
that's easy--

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:28.733
never change anything.

00:30:32.228 --> 00:30:33.710
I exaggerate, of course.

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:36.110
Even then, depending
on your service,

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:37.470
you still wouldn't have 100%.

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.110
But the point is
every change you

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:42.860
make to your service
carries some risk with it.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:46.077
But how do you decide if
that risk is worth it?

00:30:46.077 --> 00:30:47.660
You know, this is a
fantastic feature.

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:48.950
We absolutely have to ship it.

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:52.220
We have to ship it tomorrow.

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:55.340
How do you think about the
risk of shipping that feature?

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:59.580
Well, have you met
your SLO this month?

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:02.670
Do you have some
error budget leftover?

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:04.459
We talk about SLO
and error budget.

00:31:04.459 --> 00:31:06.250
They're sort of two
sides of the same coin.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:09.990
If you have, say, a
99.9% SLO, that means

00:31:09.990 --> 00:31:13.080
you have a 0.1% error budget.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:15.360
If you haven't served
any errors this month,

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.970
yeah, it's risky to
push this new version,

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:21.330
but if something bad
happens, well, you'll just

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:23.310
eat into your error budget.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:25.560
That's what it's there for.

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:28.350
On the other hand, if you've
had problems this month

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:31.020
and you've already
exhausted your error budget,

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:34.110
give your users a break
and wait until you've

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:36.300
had some periods
of high reliability

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:39.270
and you've replenished your
error budget before you

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:41.460
push that change.

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:43.350
It also reflects on
things like well,

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.570
how do you push your changes?

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:51.310
One of the great
recipes for how to have

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:53.130
a wonderful global
extended outage

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:56.490
is to push a change
everywhere all at once

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.260
and have no rollback plan.

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:00.570
That's a great recipe for
an extended global outage

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:02.010
to your service.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:05.970
Instead, if you do your
rollouts progressively,

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:08.290
so you push it a
little bit over here,

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:11.880
wait, monitor, see if
anything goes wrong,

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:13.770
push a little bit
more, wait, monitor,

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.230
see if anything goes
wrong, and so on and so on,

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:19.050
you could do this over
a period of hours,

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:21.510
over a period of days,
over a period of weeks,

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:26.910
even, depending on what
SLO are you trying to make.

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:29.370
What is the right pace
to push out your changes?

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:32.670
That depends on your SLO.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.710
All of these questions--
like, I can't tell you

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:40.740
whether minutes, days, or
weeks is the right pace

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:43.080
for your rollout.

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.805
You can only answer that based
on what SLO you're going for,

00:32:45.805 --> 00:32:47.430
and that will guide
you in the decision

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:50.640
to what kind of rollout and what
pace of rollout you need to do.

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:52.492
And then again, do
I absolutely need

00:32:52.492 --> 00:32:54.180
to have a rollback
plan for this?

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:57.402
Well, the now SRE that
lives on my shoulder says,

00:32:57.402 --> 00:32:58.860
of course you need
a rollback plan.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.750
That's the most important
thing you can have at rollout.

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:05.590
But looking at it objectively,
if you have very low SLO

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:11.010
and you can basically
re-push your entire service

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:15.210
in a couple of hours, and a
couple-of-hour outage is well

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:17.970
within your SLO, then
I guess you could

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:20.050
push without a rollback plan.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:22.680
But one of the best ways
to improve your reliability

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.800
is not to never break anything,
but to make sure you can always

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:27.390
fix it quickly, right?

00:33:27.390 --> 00:33:33.300
Because outages that
are measured in minutes

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.340
are not as painful
as outages that

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:39.790
are measured in hours
or, heaven forbid, days.

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:45.990
So having great, well-tested
rollback plans and rollback

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:48.910
tools such that yeah, sure, I'm
pushing this thing and I pushed

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:51.540
it here and then I waited 24
hours and then I pushed it here

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:53.460
again, I waited
another 24 hours,

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:55.260
and then I saw
something was wrong--

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:58.260
well, now I can
roll back, and not

00:33:58.260 --> 00:34:01.530
that many people were
affected to begin with.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:04.640
And we were able to
mitigate it really quickly.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:05.490
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:09.460
Well, we're definitely
coming up on time.

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:12.300
We could talk about
this for hours on end.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.179
Before we finish up, is there
anything we haven't covered,

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:17.730
or maybe, like what
you're next up to.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:20.380
What are you doing next
after this rotation?

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.010
PAUL NEWSON: So let's see.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:26.130
If you like what I have
to say about this topic

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:28.620
and you didn't find a half
an hour of me, you know,

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.000
rambling on about
it to be enough,

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:36.239
I'm hoping to put
together a talk for NEXT.

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:39.540
So come to the conference,
come see my talk.

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:44.639
And I also hope to basically
take this message on the road

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:51.356
and get out to other events
and talk about these things.

00:34:51.356 --> 00:34:52.980
The other thing that
I'm hoping to do--

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:55.500
I had a goal, and I
think I spoke about it

00:34:55.500 --> 00:35:00.300
in the first podcast, to almost
write a diary of my mission

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:01.200
control rotation.

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:02.340
I thought it would
be really cool

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:03.756
if people could
sort of play along

00:35:03.756 --> 00:35:06.000
with me as I went through.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:10.560
Turns out being an SRE can
be rather all-consuming,

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:18.690
so my series of blog posts
turned into two blog posts.

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:20.640
But I have another
one on the way,

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:23.370
and I'm hoping to continue to do
more because these insights are

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:25.380
pretty timeless.

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:29.910
So keep an eye on the
Google Cloud Platform blog,

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:33.090
and hopefully you'll
see some things from me

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:36.160
there on this topic.

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:39.240
And the other thing
I'm hoping to do

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:43.230
is I now feel like I have a
very good understanding of how

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:45.380
Google approaches this.

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:48.471
One of my hopes for going
out into the community

00:35:48.471 --> 00:35:50.970
and going to these conferences
and talking about this is not

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.760
just to stand there
and say, hey, this

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:54.690
is how Google does it.

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:55.981
You should do it this way, too.

00:35:55.981 --> 00:35:58.180
But I want to learn a
lot more about well,

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.580
how do people who
aren't Google do this?

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:02.020
Like, there's a
broader community

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:06.410
out there doing things that they
call Ops or DevOps or sometimes

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:09.820
SRE, and I want
to understand what

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:11.620
does this look like
if you're not Google?

00:36:11.620 --> 00:36:13.494
Because I believe that'll
be really important

00:36:13.494 --> 00:36:19.510
for us to guide our customers
to do the right things.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:22.450
Like, some of the things that
we do at the scale we operate at

00:36:22.450 --> 00:36:23.410
may not apply.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:26.630
Some of them may
to our customers.

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:31.480
The other thing
I'll do is just give

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:36.220
a quick mention of this program
we have now called Customer

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:38.860
Reliability Engineering.

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:45.550
This is essentially SREs who
work directly with customers.

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:46.720
It's a very small program.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:47.840
It's just getting started.

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:48.986
We wrote a blog post on it.

00:36:48.986 --> 00:36:50.860
I won't say any more
about it because I'm not

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:53.050
an expert on it, but I
just want to make you aware

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:53.800
that it exists.

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:55.656
You should go read
the blog post.

00:36:55.656 --> 00:36:57.280
