00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:06.439
All right, so let's get stuck
into the question of the week.

00:38:06.439 --> 00:38:07.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let's do that.

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:10.580
MARK MANDEL: So we're
talking about App Engine.

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:13.370
So if you're using App Engine--

00:38:13.370 --> 00:38:16.460
and so App Engine allows you
to have multiple versions

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:18.680
of an app up in the
cloud and running, which

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:20.420
is really, really useful.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:23.540
But if I'm using
an App Engine and I

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:25.814
want to see which version
of my service that I have up

00:38:25.814 --> 00:38:27.980
and running in the cloud,
which one's default, which

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.396
is the one that everyone is
seeing when they access, like,

00:38:30.396 --> 00:38:33.754
my usual URL, Francesc,
can you help me out?

00:38:33.754 --> 00:38:34.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:38:34.670 --> 00:38:38.030
So there's a couple things
about this question.

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:40.040
So first, I think the
important thing is

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:43.940
to understand that inside of a
single App Engine application,

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:46.460
you can have actually
many services,

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:50.750
which is what we
used to call modules.

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:51.960
Which are the same thing.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:53.930
We just changed the
name, basically.

00:38:53.930 --> 00:38:55.477
And every single
one of those modules

00:38:55.477 --> 00:38:57.560
can be written in a
completely different language.

00:38:57.560 --> 00:38:58.890
So it can be cool.

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:01.501
You can have some
Python and then some Go

00:39:01.501 --> 00:39:02.750
and something written in Java.

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:04.340
MARK MANDEL: We do that
on the podcast, actually.

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:05.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
We do, absolutely,

00:39:05.570 --> 00:39:07.736
because we have one module
in Go and one model which

00:39:07.736 --> 00:39:10.520
is static content, so
they're independent.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:13.250
And then inside of each
one of those modules,

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:14.882
you can have many versions.

00:39:14.882 --> 00:39:16.340
And that is very
useful, especially

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:19.610
when you're doing AB testing
or just testing, basically.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:21.540
If you want to have
something tested

00:39:21.540 --> 00:39:25.580
in prod, which is not a
great idea, but we all do it.

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.320
So you're going to
deploy it and then

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.270
make sure it actually
works before you send

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:31.090
all your customers
to that and new web

00:39:31.090 --> 00:39:32.990
page or that new service.

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:37.880
So before, we actually had that
concept of the default version,

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:41.420
meaning that if you did not
specify which version you

00:39:41.420 --> 00:39:43.970
were going to target,
then you could

00:39:43.970 --> 00:39:46.180
target that default version.

00:39:46.180 --> 00:39:48.470
And that thing
actually went away.

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:50.690
There's no such thing
as a default version.

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:54.530
Instead what we have is
we have traffic splitting,

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:57.530
which means that you could
do something like actual AB

00:39:57.530 --> 00:39:59.210
testing, saying,
you know, I'm going

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:02.120
to have 50% of the traffic
going to version A,

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:06.520
then 25% going to version B, and
another 25% going to version C.

00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:09.020
MARK MANDEL: So I would also,
like, smoke test new features.

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:12.124
Like, 10% of the traffic gets
a new feature, test it out,

00:40:12.124 --> 00:40:13.790
make sure it works,
and then if it does,

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:16.010
I can just increase the
traffic that it receives.

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:17.176
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Absolutely.

00:40:17.176 --> 00:40:19.460
You can do AB testing, you
can do, like, experiments,

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:21.790
and see, like, oh,
only 1% of the people

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:24.800
hitting the web page are going
to see this new feature to see

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:28.070
if they like it or not.

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:31.910
That means, basically, that
you cannot check which one is

00:40:31.910 --> 00:40:34.430
the default version
of a service.

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:38.900
Instead what you can
do is you can list

00:40:38.900 --> 00:40:40.760
all the versions in a service.

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:44.450
And when you do that, which
there's a REST API called

00:40:44.450 --> 00:40:46.190
apps.service/get.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:48.200
So you do a REST
API there, and you

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:51.230
get a list of all the versions
running in the service,

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:54.200
and which is the traffic
split that they got.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.300
So the [INAUDIBLE]
of default would

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:59.560
be 1, which is 100%
of the traffic.

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:02.787
But you could get, like,
as we're saying, 0.5, 0.25,

00:41:02.787 --> 00:41:03.870
or whatever else you want.

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:10.609
MARK MANDEL: So it's not
quite as simple as the default

00:41:10.609 --> 00:41:12.150
[INAUDIBLE] option
used to be, but is

00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:14.640
much more flexible in terms
of what you can actually do.

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:14.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:16.050
Basically, it is.

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:19.230
You can still implement
that default version, saying

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:23.040
100% traffic goes there,
but you can do much more,

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:24.680
which is actually very good.

00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:25.930
MARK MANDEL: That sounds good.

00:41:25.930 --> 00:41:27.270
I like it.

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:27.900
